WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.042 --> 00:00:03.628
This chart shows China's 
birth and death rate 

00:00:03.628 --> 00:00:05.171
over the last 60 years.

00:00:05.505 --> 00:00:07.632
For most of it, births 
are high.

00:00:07.632 --> 00:00:11.302
But in 2022, China had 
more deaths than births

00:00:11.302 --> 00:00:13.138
causing its population 
to decrease

00:00:13.138 --> 00:00:15.515
for the first time 
in 6 decades.

00:00:15.974 --> 00:00:18.768
To get why this is such 
a big deal, consider this:

00:00:19.185 --> 00:00:21.938
China is the world's 
manufacturing superpower

00:00:21.938 --> 00:00:25.025
and that's in large part because of 
its huge population.

00:00:25.275 --> 00:00:28.319
Nearly 30% of the country's 
economic output 

00:00:28.319 --> 00:00:29.446
comes from manufacturing.

00:00:29.946 --> 00:00:32.615
Now, here's China's population 
over the last 60 years.

00:00:32.699 --> 00:00:36.244
Even after losing almost 
a million people in 2022

00:00:36.244 --> 00:00:39.080
its population is still about 
as big as it's ever been:

00:00:39.080 --> 00:00:40.957
1.4 billion people.

00:00:41.374 --> 00:00:43.752
But that's projected to shrink 
by nearly half

00:00:43.752 --> 00:00:45.670
by the end of the century.

00:00:45.670 --> 00:00:48.006
Over the years, China's growth 
and policies

00:00:48.006 --> 00:00:50.258
have contributed to 
its population decline.

00:00:50.675 --> 00:00:52.635
Today, it's looking to 
reverse course

00:00:52.635 --> 00:00:54.429
to keep its population steady.

00:00:54.429 --> 00:00:57.432
The problem is 
it might be too late.

00:00:59.768 --> 00:01:02.020
In the 50s, under Mao,
China experienced

00:01:02.020 --> 00:01:04.314
one of the most gruesome 
famines on record.

00:01:04.939 --> 00:01:07.067
30 million people died.

00:01:07.734 --> 00:01:10.028
If we look at that on the 
birth and death rates chart

00:01:10.028 --> 00:01:11.571
you'll see a big spike in deaths.

00:01:11.988 --> 00:01:14.240
At the same time, the 
birth rate dropped

00:01:14.240 --> 00:01:16.367
causing the population 
to shrink.

00:01:16.367 --> 00:01:20.830
But, as often happens with wars, famines,
and other major crises

00:01:20.830 --> 00:01:23.083
immediately after, there was 
a baby boom.

00:01:23.500 --> 00:01:25.335
Combined with global 
medical advances

00:01:25.335 --> 00:01:27.545
that decreased infant 
mortality rates

00:01:27.545 --> 00:01:30.131
China's average family 
now had 6 children.

00:01:30.340 --> 00:01:32.175
The birth rate had skyrocketed

00:01:32.175 --> 00:01:34.260
which the government saw 
as a big problem.

00:01:34.469 --> 00:01:36.763
The Chinese leadership realized

00:01:36.763 --> 00:01:38.556
the population was 
growing too fast

00:01:38.556 --> 00:01:40.642
and something needs 
to be done.

00:01:40.642 --> 00:01:43.520
The government came out 
with a policy...

00:01:43.686 --> 00:01:45.730
They called it “Later, Longer, Fewer”.

00:01:45.855 --> 00:01:50.026
Later marriages, longer birth intervals, 
and fewer births.

00:01:50.568 --> 00:01:53.321
As a result, China's birth rate 
started trending down...

00:01:53.530 --> 00:01:55.824
but it wasn't low enough 
for China's leaders.

00:01:55.824 --> 00:02:00.078
And in 1980, they implemented 
the extreme one child policy

00:02:00.078 --> 00:02:02.539
which limited most families 
to one child.

00:02:02.831 --> 00:02:07.085
That policy was also backed up 
by very harsh measures.

00:02:07.335 --> 00:02:10.046
There were campaigns 
of sterilization...

00:02:10.213 --> 00:02:12.966
IUD insertion and 
induced abortions.

00:02:13.133 --> 00:02:16.719
And while these campaigns began 
during the Later, Longer, Fewer era

00:02:16.719 --> 00:02:19.055
they were at their worst 
under the one child policy

00:02:19.055 --> 00:02:22.058
when China sterilized 20 million 
men and women

00:02:22.058 --> 00:02:25.603
and induced nearly 15 million 
abortions in a single year.

00:02:26.604 --> 00:02:28.356
But China had accomplished 
its goal.

00:02:28.356 --> 00:02:30.650
Population growth was under control.

00:02:30.650 --> 00:02:33.069
Except, as China would soon realize

00:02:33.069 --> 00:02:36.793
these restrictive policies 
worked a little too well.

00:02:38.783 --> 00:02:40.535
In order for any population

00:02:40.535 --> 00:02:42.412
to stay the same size 
in the long run

00:02:42.412 --> 00:02:45.790
each couple needs to have, 
on average, 2.1 children.

00:02:46.457 --> 00:02:48.334
This is called 
the replacement rate.

00:02:48.334 --> 00:02:51.421
The idea is that one child 
replaces one parent

00:02:51.421 --> 00:02:54.215
and that 0.1 makes up for 
children who die 

00:02:54.215 --> 00:02:55.466
before they become adults.

00:02:55.842 --> 00:02:57.886
But China has had a fertility rate

00:02:57.886 --> 00:03:00.430
that's far below 2 
for over 3 decades.

00:03:01.097 --> 00:03:01.931
To bring that up

00:03:01.931 --> 00:03:05.351
in 2016, China finally ended 
the one child policy.

00:03:05.768 --> 00:03:08.861
And after briefly trying out 
a three child policy,

00:03:08.861 --> 00:03:13.568
in 2021 they finally let families have 
as many children as they'd like.

00:03:13.902 --> 00:03:15.737
But it hasn't worked.

00:03:15.737 --> 00:03:18.198
One big reason is the 
unique family structure

00:03:18.198 --> 00:03:19.991
produced by the one child policy.

00:03:19.991 --> 00:03:23.578
We're looking at what's called a 
4-2-1 family structure

00:03:23.578 --> 00:03:28.750
with a couple having 4 parents above them 
and 1 child below.

00:03:29.709 --> 00:03:32.503
Most countries have diverse 
family structures

00:03:32.503 --> 00:03:34.631
some with 3 kids, 
others with none.

00:03:34.631 --> 00:03:36.716
But with China's 4-2-1 model

00:03:36.716 --> 00:03:39.677
millions of only children are under 
increasing pressure

00:03:39.677 --> 00:03:43.056
to care for their aging parents 
and elderly grandparents.

00:03:43.181 --> 00:03:46.434
And this can make having 
multiple children even harder...

00:03:46.726 --> 00:03:49.479
especially as the cost of living 
keeps rising.

00:03:50.146 --> 00:03:53.733
A recent survey revealed that more than 
50% of young people

00:03:53.733 --> 00:03:55.526
don't want more than one child

00:03:55.526 --> 00:03:57.654
because of financial and work pressures.

00:03:57.695 --> 00:04:01.991
We have seen cash subsidies 
for additional birth

00:04:01.991 --> 00:04:04.160
longer maternal leaves...

00:04:04.577 --> 00:04:07.538
subsidies for kindergarten

00:04:07.538 --> 00:04:10.583
and all sorts of monetary support.

00:04:10.583 --> 00:04:13.544
Well, the thing is, almost none of them 
have worked

00:04:13.544 --> 00:04:16.547
because having a child is 
exceedingly expensive

00:04:16.547 --> 00:04:19.175
and it's a lifelong commitment.

00:04:20.093 --> 00:04:22.845
And so it's really actually hard...

00:04:23.263 --> 00:04:24.973
to put a price on this.

00:04:24.973 --> 00:04:28.142
But China's population crisis 
isn't just about babies.

00:04:28.351 --> 00:04:31.479
It's also about the balance 
between young and old.

00:04:32.981 --> 00:04:36.442
If we look at population pyramids
that show distribution by age...

00:04:36.651 --> 00:04:38.278
we see that countries like Kenya

00:04:38.278 --> 00:04:40.905
with rapid population growth 
look like this:

00:04:40.905 --> 00:04:42.031
wide at the bottom

00:04:42.031 --> 00:04:45.618
representing a lot of new young people, 
and narrow at the top.

00:04:45.618 --> 00:04:47.412
Countries experiencing 
slower growth

00:04:47.412 --> 00:04:49.747
like the Philippines, 
are still triangular.

00:04:49.747 --> 00:04:52.625
But the difference between top and bottom 
is less pronounced.

00:04:52.959 --> 00:04:57.000
Now take a look at China, and notice
the narrow bottom, so, fewer babies.

00:04:57.000 --> 00:05:00.300
And the heavy top: a larger
number of elderly people.

00:05:00.300 --> 00:05:01.718
Which is a happy...

00:05:02.468 --> 00:05:05.096
outcome of our improvement

00:05:05.096 --> 00:05:07.724
in health and in standard of living

00:05:07.724 --> 00:05:11.311
but combined with sustained 
low fertility...

00:05:11.686 --> 00:05:14.981
that just produces sustained 
population aging.

00:05:14.981 --> 00:05:18.818
In 2050, that pyramid is projected 
to look like this.

00:05:19.569 --> 00:05:22.363
And that will further drive down 
China's population

00:05:22.363 --> 00:05:25.074
shrink its labor force and 
put the whole country

00:05:25.074 --> 00:05:27.869
in a uniquely difficult position.

00:05:30.371 --> 00:05:33.666
In the 80s, China became a hotspot 
for foreign investment

00:05:33.666 --> 00:05:35.752
cheap manufacturing 
and exports.

00:05:36.461 --> 00:05:38.796
A generation later, it was 
shooting up the ranks

00:05:38.796 --> 00:05:40.340
and becoming one of the world's leading

00:05:40.340 --> 00:05:42.800
and fastest growing economies by GDP.

00:05:43.092 --> 00:05:45.553
But not only did that economic modernization

00:05:45.553 --> 00:05:48.723
drive birthrates down further, 
it also didn't

00:05:48.723 --> 00:05:51.726
translate to an equally strong economy 
for everyone.

00:05:51.851 --> 00:05:53.811
If we look at the GDP per capita

00:05:53.811 --> 00:05:56.356
the best indicator we have 
for standard of living

00:05:56.356 --> 00:05:59.275
China is much lower than these 
high income countries.

00:06:00.360 --> 00:06:03.696
China became a major world economy 
nearly overnight

00:06:03.696 --> 00:06:05.865
but it's still a middle income country.

00:06:06.783 --> 00:06:08.576
Many, especially in rural areas

00:06:08.576 --> 00:06:11.162
haven't benefited much from 
China's economic boom...

00:06:11.746 --> 00:06:14.874
and China has yet to develop 
the necessary safety nets

00:06:14.874 --> 00:06:16.667
to support its aging population.

00:06:17.085 --> 00:06:19.629
To build the social infrastructure...

00:06:20.004 --> 00:06:25.301
like the social programs in 
health care and in pensions...

00:06:26.260 --> 00:06:27.220
It takes time.

00:06:27.220 --> 00:06:29.389
And that's getting...

00:06:29.472 --> 00:06:32.475
actually tougher with the economy 
that's slowing down.

00:06:32.600 --> 00:06:35.603
And a slower economy will inevitably redefine

00:06:35.603 --> 00:06:38.856
China's role in the world as 
a manufacturing superpower.

00:06:39.148 --> 00:06:40.983
What this means for China,
for the world

00:06:40.983 --> 00:06:43.945
is that the resource constraints 
from within...

00:06:44.654 --> 00:06:48.157
would also constrain Chinese ambition...

00:06:48.783 --> 00:06:51.411
and its global reach.

00:06:52.453 --> 00:06:54.914
In some ways, China 
isn't alone.

00:06:54.914 --> 00:06:56.833
A lot of Asian and European countries

00:06:56.833 --> 00:06:59.168
are experiencing population declines, too.

00:06:59.710 --> 00:07:02.964
What makes China different is how fast
this all has happened.

00:07:03.464 --> 00:07:04.966
It was only 40 years ago

00:07:04.966 --> 00:07:07.635
that China started leveraging 
its booming population

00:07:07.635 --> 00:07:09.679
to become an economic superpower...

00:07:10.096 --> 00:07:12.682
all while still trying to stem 
population growth.

00:07:13.057 --> 00:07:16.185
Now that China's population growth 
is officially over...

00:07:16.310 --> 00:07:18.229
China may have to rethink its future

00:07:18.229 --> 00:07:19.981
not just as a global superpower

00:07:19.981 --> 00:07:22.942
but for its citizens 
at home too.

